In a hybrid business environment, your internal servers and Azure cloud pipeline transmit three primary types of data:
Identity and Access Tokens (Security Traffic): Every time an employee logs in, the server transmits encrypted security
tokens between your local network and the Microsoft Tenant. This checks permissions and instantly approves or blocks
access to emails, files, or cloud apps.Delta-File Changes (Storage Traffic): Instead of uploading massive file duplicates,
your server utilizes Azure File Sync to transmit tiny "deltas" (only the exact characters or pixels that changed in a 
document) to your Azure cloud backup.Administrative State Telemetry (Management Traffic): Tools like Azure Arc transmit 
lightweight metadata packets. These do not contain private customer data; instead, they transmit system health reports,
CPU temperatures, pending security patch alerts, and system logs back to your central dashboard.2. What Potential 
Business Goals Are Reached?By structuring your infrastructure this way, your business achieves three critical operational
milestones:Zero-Trust Security Baseline: You eliminate weak, shared office passwords. If an employee's laptop is 
stolen, you can revoke their access across the entire Microsoft Tenant instantly from a single button, locking down all 
internal servers simultaneously.Infrastructure Cost Optimization: You avoid purchasing expensive physical hard drive array
s every year. Your server acts as a smart cache, keeping active files local and offloading archive data to cheap cloud
tiers automatically.High Operational Uptime: If your primary office loses power or internet, your critical workflows
